(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("ansible",{"ansible/ansible-basics-inventory-and-ad-hoc":{"html":"\u003cp>You have twelve servers that should all have the same web server, the same users and the same firewall rules. Logging in to each one works for a week. After that, one server has a different package version, one has a forgotten manual change, and nobody can say which. Configuration management keeps servers matching a description instead of matching someone&#39;s memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What configuration management solves, and where Ansible fits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How Ansible reaches hosts without installing an agent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing an inventory with groups and variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running ad-hoc commands and modules\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-ansible-works\">How Ansible works\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Ansible runs on a \u003cstrong>control node\u003c/strong> (your laptop or a build server). It connects to \u003cstrong>managed nodes\u003c/strong> over SSH (or WinRM for Windows), copies a small module to each, runs it, reads the result and removes it. Nothing needs to be installed on the managed nodes except Python and an SSH login.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Agentless\u003c/strong>: no daemon to patch or secure on each server\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Declarative modules\u003c/strong>: you say &quot;package nginx is present&quot;, not &quot;run apt install&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Push model\u003c/strong>: you decide when changes happen, which suits controlled rollouts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Term\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Control node\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The machine that runs \u003ccode>ansible\u003c/code> commands\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Managed node\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A server Ansible configures\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Inventory\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The list of hosts and groups\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Module\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A unit of work such as \u003ccode>apt\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>copy\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>service\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Playbook\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A YAML file that lists tasks to run (next lesson)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-inventory\">The inventory\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An inventory names your hosts and sorts them into groups. The INI form is the quickest to read.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[web]\nweb1.example.com\nweb2.example.com ansible_user=deploy\n\n[db]\ndb1.example.com\n\n[prod:children]\nweb\ndb\n\n[prod:vars]\nansible_python_interpreter=/usr/bin/python3\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">[web]\nweb1.example.com\nweb2.example.com ansible_user=deploy\n\n[db]\ndb1.example.com\n\n[prod:children]\nweb\ndb\n\n[prod:vars]\nansible_python_interpreter=/usr/bin/python3\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>[web]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[db]\u003c/code> are groups of hosts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>[prod:children]\u003c/code> makes \u003ccode>prod\u003c/code> a group made of other groups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>[prod:vars]\u003c/code> sets variables for every host in \u003ccode>prod\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Check what Ansible sees before you change anything:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --graph\nansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --host web2.example.com\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --graph\nansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --host web2.example.com\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Cloud environments change constantly, so teams often use a \u003cstrong>dynamic inventory\u003c/strong> plugin that asks the cloud API for current instances instead of a hand-written file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ad-hoc-commands\">Ad-hoc commands\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An ad-hoc command runs one module on a set of hosts. It is ideal for quick facts and one-off fixes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping\nansible web -i inventory.ini -m command -a &quot;uptime&quot;\nansible web -i inventory.ini -m apt -a &quot;name=nginx state=present&quot; --become\nansible db -i inventory.ini -m service -a &quot;name=postgresql state=started&quot; --become\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping\nansible web -i inventory.ini -m \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">command\u003c/span> -a \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;uptime&quot;\u003c/span>\nansible web -i inventory.ini -m apt -a \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name=nginx state=present&quot;\u003c/span> --become\nansible db -i inventory.ini -m service -a \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name=postgresql state=started&quot;\u003c/span> --become\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>-m\u003c/code> chooses the module and \u003ccode>-a\u003c/code> gives its arguments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--become\u003c/code> runs with elevated rights (sudo)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--check\u003c/code> shows what would change without changing it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--limit web1.example.com\u003c/code> restricts the run to part of the inventory\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>ping\u003c/code> module is not an ICMP ping. It checks that SSH works and Python is usable on the target.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-modules-beat-shell-commands\">Why modules beat shell commands\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>state=present\u003c/code> can be run ten times and install the package once. The module reports \u003ccode>changed\u003c/code> only when it altered something. A raw \u003ccode>command: apt install nginx\u003c/code> has no such awareness. Use a module when one exists, and reserve \u003ccode>command\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>shell\u003c/code> for the cases that have none.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You run the same ad-hoc apt module with state=present twice against a host that has no nginx. What do the two runs report?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The first run reports changed (it installed nginx). The second reports ok with no change, because the package is already present.\u003c/p>\u003cp>That &quot;changed or ok&quot; signal is idempotence, the property that makes Ansible safe to repeat.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"ctl&gt;ssh;ssh&gt;mod;mod&gt;res\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">What happens when you run an Ansible command\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Control node\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ssh\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">SSH connection\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mod\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Module copied and run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"res\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">JSON result\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Control node to SSH connection; 2, SSH connection to Module copied and run; 3, Module copied and run to JSON result.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctl\">\u003cdt>Control node \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reads the inventory and builds a connection per host.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the control node is lost, so is your only copy of the automation unless it is stored in version control.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ssh\">\u003cdt>SSH connection \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Opens an SSH session to each managed node.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong keys, firewalls or host key prompts stop the run for that host.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mod\">\u003cdt>Module copied and run \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A small Python module runs on the target and does one job.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing Python on the target gives a module failure, not a config error.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"res\">\u003cdt>JSON result \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The module returns ok, changed or failed, and is then removed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring &quot;failed&quot; on one host can leave the fleet inconsistent.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-one-server-nobody-could-rebuild\">Incident: the one server nobody could rebuild\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A payment web server died after a disk fault, and no one could recreate it because years of manual changes lived only on that machine.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ssh web3 'dpkg -l | wc -l'\nssh web1 'dpkg -l | wc -l'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ssh web3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;dpkg -l | wc -l&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">611\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ssh web1 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;dpkg -l | wc -l&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">498\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team spent two days comparing package lists. Afterwards they described the web tier in an inventory and a playbook, ran it against all servers, and made &quot;no manual changes on servers&quot; a rule. The next failure took twenty minutes to replace.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>shell\u003c/code> for everything, which gives up change reporting\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting \u003ccode>--become\u003c/code> and then misreading permission errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping the inventory only on one laptop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating \u003ccode>ping\u003c/code> as a network test\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"how-ansible-works","text":"How Ansible works"},{"id":"the-inventory","text":"The inventory"},{"id":"ad-hoc-commands","text":"Ad-hoc commands"},{"id":"why-modules-beat-shell-commands","text":"Why modules beat shell commands"},{"id":"incident-the-one-server-nobody-could-rebuild","text":"Incident: the one server nobody could rebuild"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Ansible getting started","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/getting_started/index.html"},{"t":"Building an inventory","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/inventory_guide/intro_inventory.html"},{"t":"Introduction to ad hoc commands","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/command_guide/intro_adhoc.html"}],"text":"You have twelve servers that should all have the same web server, the same users and the same firewall rules. Logging in to each one works for a week. After that, one server has a different package version, one has a forgotten manual change, and nobody can say which. Configuration management keeps servers matching a description instead of matching someone&#39;s memory. What you will learn What configuration management solves, and where Ansible fits How Ansible reaches hosts without installing an agent Writing an inventory with groups and variables Running ad-hoc commands and modules How Ansible works Ansible runs on a control node (your laptop or a build server). It connects to managed nodes over SSH (or WinRM for Windows), copies a small module to each, runs it, reads the result and removes it. Nothing needs to be installed on the managed nodes except Python and an SSH login. Agentless : no daemon to patch or secure on each server Declarative modules : you say \"package nginx is present\", not \"run apt install\" Push model : you decide when changes happen, which suits controlled rollouts Term Meaning Control node The machine that runs ansible commands Managed node A server Ansible configures Inventory The list of hosts and groups Module A unit of work such as apt , copy or service Playbook A YAML file that lists tasks to run (next lesson) The inventory An inventory names your hosts and sorts them into groups. The INI form is the quickest to read. [web] web1.example.com web2.example.com ansible_user=deploy [db] db1.example.com [prod:children] web db [prod:vars] ansible_python_interpreter=/usr/bin/python3 [web] and [db] are groups of hosts [prod:children] makes prod a group made of other groups [prod:vars] sets variables for every host in prod Check what Ansible sees before you change anything: ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --graph ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --host web2.example.com Cloud environments change constantly, so teams often use a dynamic inventory plugin that asks the cloud API for current instances instead of a hand-written file. Ad-hoc commands An ad-hoc command runs one module on a set of hosts. It is ideal for quick facts and one-off fixes. ansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping ansible web -i inventory.ini -m command -a \"uptime\" ansible web -i inventory.ini -m apt -a \"name=nginx state=present\" --become ansible db -i inventory.ini -m service -a \"name=postgresql state=started\" --become -m chooses the module and -a gives its arguments --become runs with elevated rights (sudo) --check shows what would change without changing it --limit web1.example.com restricts the run to part of the inventory The ping module is not an ICMP ping. It checks that SSH works and Python is usable on the target. Why modules beat shell commands state=present can be run ten times and install the package once. The module reports changed only when it altered something. A raw command: apt install nginx has no such awareness. Use a module when one exists, and reserve command or shell for the cases that have none. Predict first. You run the same ad-hoc apt module with state=present twice against a host that has no nginx. What do the two runs report? Your guess Answer: The first run reports changed (it installed nginx). The second reports ok with no change, because the package is already present. That \"changed or ok\" signal is idempotence, the property that makes Ansible safe to repeat. What happens when you run an Ansible command Flow in order: 1, Control node to SSH connection; 2, SSH connection to Module copied and run; 3, Module copied and run to JSON result. Service Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Control node Service What it does: Reads the inventory and builds a connection per host. If it fails or is weak: If the control node is lost, so is your only copy of the automation unless it is stored in version control. SSH connection Step What it does: "},"ansible/playbooks-tasks-and-idempotence":{"html":"\u003cp>Ad-hoc commands fix one thing once. A playbook records the whole intended state of a server in a file you can review, run again and keep in version control. It is the central artifact of Ansible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The structure of a playbook: plays, hosts, tasks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using common modules for packages, files, services and users\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Idempotence and how to read \u003ccode>changed\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>ok\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running with \u003ccode>--check\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--diff\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--limit\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-first-playbook\">A first playbook\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Configure web servers\n  hosts: web\n  become: true\n  tasks:\n    - name: Install nginx\n      ansible.builtin.apt:\n        name: nginx\n        state: present\n        update_cache: true\n\n    - name: Create the site directory\n      ansible.builtin.file:\n        path: /var/www/shop\n        state: directory\n        owner: www-data\n        mode: &quot;0755&quot;\n\n    - name: Ensure nginx is running and starts on boot\n      ansible.builtin.service:\n        name: nginx\n        state: started\n        enabled: true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Configure\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">servers\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">become:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tasks:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Install\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.apt:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">state:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">present\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">update_cache:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Create\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">site\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">directory\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.file:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/var/www/shop\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">state:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">directory\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">owner:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">www-data\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0755&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ensure\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">is\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">running\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">and\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">starts\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">on\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">boot\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.service:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">state:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">started\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">enabled:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>play\u003c/strong> maps a group of hosts to tasks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>task\u003c/strong> calls one module with arguments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tasks run top to bottom on each host, and a failed task stops that host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>name\u003c/code> is optional but it is what you read in the output, so write good ones\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Run it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml\nansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml --check --diff\nansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml --limit web1.example.com\nansible-playbook site.yml --syntax-check\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml\nansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml --check --diff\nansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">limit\u003c/span> web1.example.com\nansible-playbook site.yml --syntax-check\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"idempotence\">Idempotence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A task is \u003cstrong>idempotent\u003c/strong> when running it again changes nothing once the desired state exists. The recap printed at the end of each run tells you:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"PLAY RECAP\nweb1 : ok=4  changed=0  unreachable=0  failed=0  skipped=0\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">PLAY RECAP\nweb1 : ok=4  changed=0  unreachable=0  failed=0  skipped=0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A healthy second run shows \u003ccode>changed=0\u003c/code>. If it keeps reporting changes, something in your playbook is not describing a state, and it will hide real changes later.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Common causes of fake changes are \u003ccode>command\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>shell\u003c/code> tasks, which always report \u003ccode>changed\u003c/code>. Fix them in one of three ways:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Initialise the application once\n  ansible.builtin.command: /opt/shop/bin/init-db\n  args:\n    creates: /opt/shop/.initialised\n\n- name: Read the version without claiming a change\n  ansible.builtin.command: /opt/shop/bin/version\n  register: shop_version\n  changed_when: false\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Initialise\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">application\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">once\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.command:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/opt/shop/bin/init-db\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">args:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">creates:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/opt/shop/.initialised\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Read\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">version\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">without\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">claiming\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">a\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">change\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.command:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/opt/shop/bin/version\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">register:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop_version\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">changed_when:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>creates\u003c/code> skips the task when a file exists, and \u003ccode>changed_when: false\u003c/code> marks a read-only task as unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"useful-modules\">Useful modules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Need\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Module\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Packages\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ansible.builtin.apt\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>dnf\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>package\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Files and directories\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>file\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>copy\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>template\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>lineinfile\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Services\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>service\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>systemd\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Users and keys\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>user\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>authorized_key\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Run something\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>command\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>shell\u003c/code> (last resort)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Download\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>get_url\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>unarchive\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use the full name such as \u003ccode>ansible.builtin.apt\u003c/code>. It avoids ambiguity when collections from other sources define modules with the same short name.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-mode-and-diff\">Check mode and diff\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--check\u003c/code> runs the play without changing anything, and \u003ccode>--diff\u003c/code> prints what would change in files. Together they act as the \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> step of Ansible. Not every module can predict its effect, so a clean check is good evidence but not a guarantee.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A playbook has a shell task running &quot;echo done &gt; /tmp/flag&quot;. You run it three times. What does the recap say on each run?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> changed=1 each time, because shell always reports changed unless you add creates or changed_when.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Idempotence is a property you build with the right module or conditions, not something Ansible guesses.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"play&gt;gather;gather&gt;task;task&gt;recap\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Anatomy of a playbook run\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"play\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Play\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gather\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Gather facts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"task\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Tasks in order\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"recap\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Recap\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Play to Gather facts; 2, Gather facts to Tasks in order; 3, Tasks in order to Recap.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"play\">\u003cdt>Play \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Picks the hosts and sets options such as become.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong hosts pattern runs on too many or too few servers.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gather\">\u003cdt>Gather facts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Collects OS and hardware details from each host.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow on large fleets, and fails if Python is missing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"task\">\u003cdt>Tasks in order \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Each task calls a module and reports ok, changed or failed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A failed task stops the host, leaving it half configured.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"recap\">\u003cdt>Recap \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Counts of ok, changed, failed and unreachable per host.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unread recaps hide hosts that silently failed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-playbook-that-restarted-production-every-run\">Incident: the playbook that restarted production every run\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team scheduled a playbook every hour. Because one task always reported changed, a handler restarted the application hourly and caused short outages.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-playbook site.yml | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-playbook site.yml | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web1 : ok=9  changed=1  unreachable=0  failed=0  skipped=0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web2 : ok=9  changed=1  unreachable=0  failed=0  skipped=0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003ccode>shell\u003c/code> task that wrote a timestamp into a config file was reporting a change every time. They replaced it with a \u003ccode>template\u003c/code> task that renders the same content when nothing changes, and added a pipeline check that fails when a second run is not clean.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>shell\u003c/code> where a module exists\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the second run that proves idempotence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving tasks unnamed so output is unreadable\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a whole play when \u003ccode>--limit\u003c/code> would test it on one host first\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-first-playbook","text":"A first playbook"},{"id":"idempotence","text":"Idempotence"},{"id":"useful-modules","text":"Useful modules"},{"id":"check-mode-and-diff","text":"Check mode and diff"},{"id":"incident-the-playbook-that-restarted-production-every-run","text":"Incident: the playbook that restarted production every run"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Ansible playbooks","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_intro.html"},{"t":"Validating tasks: check mode and diff mode","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_checkmode.html"},{"t":"Working with playbooks","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/index.html"}],"text":"Ad-hoc commands fix one thing once. A playbook records the whole intended state of a server in a file you can review, run again and keep in version control. It is the central artifact of Ansible. What you will learn The structure of a playbook: plays, hosts, tasks Using common modules for packages, files, services and users Idempotence and how to read changed and ok Running with --check , --diff and --limit A first playbook - name: Configure web servers hosts: web become: true tasks: - name: Install nginx ansible.builtin.apt: name: nginx state: present update_cache: true - name: Create the site directory ansible.builtin.file: path: /var/www/shop state: directory owner: www-data mode: \"0755\" - name: Ensure nginx is running and starts on boot ansible.builtin.service: name: nginx state: started enabled: true A play maps a group of hosts to tasks A task calls one module with arguments Tasks run top to bottom on each host, and a failed task stops that host name is optional but it is what you read in the output, so write good ones Run it: ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml --check --diff ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml -- limit web1.example.com ansible-playbook site.yml --syntax-check Idempotence A task is idempotent when running it again changes nothing once the desired state exists. The recap printed at the end of each run tells you: PLAY RECAP web1 : ok=4 changed=0 unreachable=0 failed=0 skipped=0 A healthy second run shows changed=0 . If it keeps reporting changes, something in your playbook is not describing a state, and it will hide real changes later. Common causes of fake changes are command or shell tasks, which always report changed . Fix them in one of three ways: - name: Initialise the application once ansible.builtin.command: /opt/shop/bin/init-db args: creates: /opt/shop/.initialised - name: Read the version without claiming a change ansible.builtin.command: /opt/shop/bin/version register: shop_version changed_when: false creates skips the task when a file exists, and changed_when: false marks a read-only task as unchanged. Useful modules Need Module Packages ansible.builtin.apt , dnf , package Files and directories file , copy , template , lineinfile Services service , systemd Users and keys user , authorized_key Run something command , shell (last resort) Download get_url , unarchive Use the full name such as ansible.builtin.apt . It avoids ambiguity when collections from other sources define modules with the same short name. Check mode and diff --check runs the play without changing anything, and --diff prints what would change in files. Together they act as the plan step of Ansible. Not every module can predict its effect, so a clean check is good evidence but not a guarantee. Predict first. A playbook has a shell task running \"echo done > /tmp/flag\". You run it three times. What does the recap say on each run? Your guess Answer: changed=1 each time, because shell always reports changed unless you add creates or changed_when. Idempotence is a property you build with the right module or conditions, not something Ansible guesses. Anatomy of a playbook run Flow in order: 1, Play to Gather facts; 2, Gather facts to Tasks in order; 3, Tasks in order to Recap. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Play Step What it does: Picks the hosts and sets options such as become. If it fails or is weak: A wrong hosts pattern runs on too many or too few servers. Gather facts Step What it does: Collects OS and hardware details from each host. If it fails or is weak: Slow on large fleets, and fails if Python is missing. Tasks in order Step What it does: Each task calls a module and reports ok, changed or failed. If it fails or is weak: A failed task stops the host, leaving it half configured. Recap Step What it does: Counts of ok, changed, failed and unreachable per host. If it fails or is weak"},"ansible/variables-facts-and-templates":{"html":"\u003cp>One playbook should configure staging and production, differing only in values such as ports, hostnames and sizes. Variables and templates let you keep the logic once and the differences in data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Defining variables in playbooks, inventory and files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Variable precedence, and why it surprises people\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Facts gathered from hosts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Jinja2 templates for configuration files\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-variables-live\">Where variables live\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Configure the app\n  hosts: web\n  become: true\n  vars:\n    app_port: 8080\n  vars_files:\n    - vars/common.yml\n  tasks:\n    - name: Show the port\n      ansible.builtin.debug:\n        msg: &quot;Listening on {{ app_port }}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Configure\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">become:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">vars:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app_port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">vars_files:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">vars/common.yml\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tasks:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Show\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">port\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.debug:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">msg:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Listening on \u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ app_port }}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Common places, from broad to specific:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>group_vars/web.yml\u003c/code> applies to every host in the \u003ccode>web\u003c/code> group\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>host_vars/web1.example.com.yml\u003c/code> applies to one host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>vars\u003c/code> in a play or \u003ccode>vars_files\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>-e &quot;app_port=9090&quot;\u003c/code> on the command line, which wins over almost everything\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Directory layout is a good habit:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"inventory/\n  production/\n    hosts.ini\n    group_vars/\n      all.yml\n      web.yml\n  staging/\n    hosts.ini\n    group_vars/\n      all.yml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">inventory/\n  production/\n    hosts.ini\n    group_vars/\n      all.yml\n      web.yml\n  staging/\n    hosts.ini\n    group_vars/\n      all.yml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each environment owns its values, and the same playbook runs against either inventory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"precedence\">Precedence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Ansible has more than twenty precedence levels. You only need the idea: \u003cstrong>more specific beats more general, and extra vars (\u003ccode>-e\u003c/code>) beat everything\u003c/strong>. To avoid confusion, define each variable in one place, give role defaults a clear name prefix, and use \u003ccode>ansible-inventory --host NAME\u003c/code> to see what a host really receives.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"facts\">Facts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before tasks run, Ansible gathers \u003cstrong>facts\u003c/strong>, details about the host stored in \u003ccode>ansible_facts\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Use facts\n  hosts: all\n  tasks:\n    - name: Report the distribution\n      ansible.builtin.debug:\n        msg: &quot;{{ ansible_facts['distribution'] }} {{ ansible_facts['distribution_version'] }}, {{ ansible_facts['memtotal_mb'] }} MB&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Use\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">facts\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">all\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tasks:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Report\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">distribution\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.debug:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">msg:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ ansible_facts[&#x27;distribution&#x27;] }}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ ansible_facts[&#x27;distribution_version&#x27;] }}\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ ansible_facts[&#x27;memtotal_mb&#x27;] }}\u003c/span> MB&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible web1.example.com -i inventory.ini -m setup -a &quot;filter=ansible_distribution*&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ansible web1.example.com -i inventory.ini -m setup -a \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;filter=ansible_distribution*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Facts let a playbook adapt to the operating system or memory size. Gathering costs time on large fleets, so set \u003ccode>gather_facts: false\u003c/code> where you do not use them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"templates\">Templates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A template is a text file with Jinja2 placeholders. The \u003ccode>template\u003c/code> module renders it on the control node and copies the result.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# nginx site for {{ site_name }}  -- managed by Ansible, do not edit by hand\nserver {\n    listen {{ app_port }};\n    server_name {{ site_name }};\n{% for upstream in app_backends %}\n    # backend {{ upstream }}\n{% endfor %}\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\"># nginx site for {{ site_name }}  -- managed by Ansible, do not edit by hand\nserver {\n    listen {{ app_port }};\n    server_name {{ site_name }};\n{% for upstream in app_backends %}\n    # backend {{ upstream }}\n{% endfor %}\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Render the site configuration\n  ansible.builtin.template:\n    src: site.conf.j2\n    dest: /etc/nginx/conf.d/shop.conf\n    owner: root\n    group: root\n    mode: &quot;0644&quot;\n    validate: &quot;nginx -t -c /etc/nginx/nginx.conf&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Render\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">site\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">configuration\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">src:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">site.conf.j2\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">dest:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/etc/nginx/conf.d/shop.conf\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">owner:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">root\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">group:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">root\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0644&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">validate:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;nginx -t -c /etc/nginx/nginx.conf&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>{{ }}\u003c/code> prints a value, \u003ccode>{% %}\u003c/code> holds logic such as loops and conditions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The header comment tells future readers not to edit by hand\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>validate\u003c/code> runs a check on the rendered file before it replaces the old one (some tools need \u003ccode>%s\u003c/code> in the command for the file path)\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Filters transform values: \u003ccode>{{ name | upper }}\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>{{ port | default(80) }}\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>{{ items | join(&#39;,&#39;) }}\u003c/code>. The \u003ccode>default\u003c/code> filter prevents failures when a variable is missing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> group_vars/all.yml sets app_port to 8080 and group_vars/web.yml sets it to 9090. A web host runs the play with no other settings. Which port does it get?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> 9090, because the group web is more specific than all. Adding -e app_port=7070 on the command line would override both.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Specific beats general, and extra vars beat everything else.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"vars&gt;render;tpl&gt;render;render&gt;dest\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How a templated file reaches a server\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vars\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Variables and facts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tpl\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Jinja2 template\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"render\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rendered on control node\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dest\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">File on the host\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Variables and facts to Rendered on control node; 2, Jinja2 template to Rendered on control node; 3, Rendered on control node to File on the host.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vars\">\u003cdt>Variables and facts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Values from inventory, group_vars, play vars and gathered facts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A value defined in two places can silently win from the wrong one.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tpl\">\u003cdt>Jinja2 template \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A text file with placeholders and simple logic.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing variables stop the play or render empty text.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"render\">\u003cdt>Rendered on control node \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The template module fills in the values.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A logic mistake only shows after rendering.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dest\">\u003cdt>File on the host \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Copied only if content differs, then validated.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An unvalidated bad file can break the service on reload.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-production-config-with-staging-values\">Incident: the production config with staging values\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deployment pointed production at the staging database because a variable defined in two places let the wrong value win.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-inventory -i inventory/production/hosts.ini --host web1 | grep db_host\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-inventory -i inventory/production/hosts.ini --host web1 | grep db_host\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    &quot;db_host&quot;: &quot;db.staging.internal&quot;,\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An old \u003ccode>-e @staging.json\u003c/code> was still in a shell alias. The team removed extra-vars files from routine use, put the database host only in \u003ccode>group_vars/production\u003c/code>, and added a check that prints the key variables for confirmation before the real run.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Defining the same variable in many places\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping \u003ccode>default\u003c/code> filters and failing on a missing value\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing the deployed file by hand and losing the change at the next run\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting secrets in plain variable files (see the vault lesson)\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-variables-live","text":"Where variables live"},{"id":"precedence","text":"Precedence"},{"id":"facts","text":"Facts"},{"id":"templates","text":"Templates"},{"id":"incident-the-production-config-with-staging-values","text":"Incident: the production config with staging values"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Using variables","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_variables.html"},{"t":"Templating with Jinja2","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_templating.html"},{"t":"Discovering variables: facts","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_vars_facts.html"}],"text":"One playbook should configure staging and production, differing only in values such as ports, hostnames and sizes. Variables and templates let you keep the logic once and the differences in data. What you will learn Defining variables in playbooks, inventory and files Variable precedence, and why it surprises people Facts gathered from hosts Jinja2 templates for configuration files Where variables live - name: Configure the app hosts: web become: true vars: app_port: 8080 vars_files: - vars/common.yml tasks: - name: Show the port ansible.builtin.debug: msg: \"Listening on {{ app_port }} \" Common places, from broad to specific: group_vars/web.yml applies to every host in the web group host_vars/web1.example.com.yml applies to one host vars in a play or vars_files -e \"app_port=9090\" on the command line, which wins over almost everything Directory layout is a good habit: inventory/ production/ hosts.ini group_vars/ all.yml web.yml staging/ hosts.ini group_vars/ all.yml Each environment owns its values, and the same playbook runs against either inventory. Precedence Ansible has more than twenty precedence levels. You only need the idea: more specific beats more general, and extra vars ( -e ) beat everything . To avoid confusion, define each variable in one place, give role defaults a clear name prefix, and use ansible-inventory --host NAME to see what a host really receives. Facts Before tasks run, Ansible gathers facts , details about the host stored in ansible_facts . - name: Use facts hosts: all tasks: - name: Report the distribution ansible.builtin.debug: msg: \" {{ ansible_facts[&#x27;distribution&#x27;] }} {{ ansible_facts[&#x27;distribution_version&#x27;] }} , {{ ansible_facts[&#x27;memtotal_mb&#x27;] }} MB\" ansible web1.example.com -i inventory.ini -m setup -a \"filter=ansible_distribution*\" Facts let a playbook adapt to the operating system or memory size. Gathering costs time on large fleets, so set gather_facts: false where you do not use them. Templates A template is a text file with Jinja2 placeholders. The template module renders it on the control node and copies the result. # nginx site for {{ site_name }} -- managed by Ansible, do not edit by hand server { listen {{ app_port }}; server_name {{ site_name }}; {% for upstream in app_backends %} # backend {{ upstream }} {% endfor %} } - name: Render the site configuration ansible.builtin.template: src: site.conf.j2 dest: /etc/nginx/conf.d/shop.conf owner: root group: root mode: \"0644\" validate: \"nginx -t -c /etc/nginx/nginx.conf\" {{ }} prints a value, {% %} holds logic such as loops and conditions The header comment tells future readers not to edit by hand validate runs a check on the rendered file before it replaces the old one (some tools need %s in the command for the file path) Filters transform values: {{ name | upper }} , {{ port | default(80) }} , {{ items | join(&#39;,&#39;) }} . The default filter prevents failures when a variable is missing. Predict first. group_vars/all.yml sets app_port to 8080 and group_vars/web.yml sets it to 9090. A web host runs the play with no other settings. Which port does it get? Your guess Answer: 9090, because the group web is more specific than all. Adding -e app_port=7070 on the command line would override both. Specific beats general, and extra vars beat everything else. How a templated file reaches a server Flow in order: 1, Variables and facts to Rendered on control node; 2, Jinja2 template to Rendered on control node; 3, Rendered on control node to File on the host. Step Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Variables and facts Step What it does: Values from inventory, group_vars, play vars and gathered facts. If it fails or is weak: A value defined in two places can silently win from the wrong one. Jinja2 template Step What it does: A text file with placeholders and simple logic. If it fails or is weak: Missing variables stop the pl"},"ansible/handlers-conditionals-and-loops":{"html":"\u003cp>Real servers differ and real changes need follow-up: reload the web server only if its config changed, install a package only on Debian family hosts, create five users without five copied tasks. These tools give a playbook that flow without turning it into a script.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>when\u003c/code> conditions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>loop\u003c/code> over lists and dictionaries\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handlers and when they run\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>block\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>rescue\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>always\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tags and limiting a run\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conditions\">Conditions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Install on Debian family\n  ansible.builtin.apt:\n    name: nginx\n    state: present\n  when: ansible_facts['os_family'] == &quot;Debian&quot;\n\n- name: Install on Red Hat family\n  ansible.builtin.dnf:\n    name: nginx\n    state: present\n  when: ansible_facts['os_family'] == &quot;RedHat&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Install\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">on\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Debian\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">family\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.apt:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">state:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">present\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">when:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ansible_facts[&#x27;os_family&#x27;]\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">==\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Debian&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Install\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">on\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Red\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Hat\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">family\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.dnf:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">state:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">present\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">when:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ansible_facts[&#x27;os_family&#x27;]\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">==\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;RedHat&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>when\u003c/code> takes a plain expression with no braces. Combine conditions with \u003ccode>and\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>or\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>not\u003c/code>, and use \u003ccode>is defined\u003c/code> to test a variable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"loops\">Loops\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Create service users\n  ansible.builtin.user:\n    name: &quot;{{ item.name }}&quot;\n    groups: &quot;{{ item.groups }}&quot;\n    state: present\n  loop:\n    - { name: alice, groups: &quot;sudo&quot; }\n    - { name: bob, groups: &quot;developers&quot; }\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Create\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">service\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">users\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.user:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ item.name }}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">groups:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ item.groups }}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">state:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">present\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">loop:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">alice\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">groups:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sudo&quot;\u003c/span> }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">bob\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">groups:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;developers&quot;\u003c/span> }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>loop_control\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>label\u003c/code> to keep output short when items are large. Do not loop over a package list with many tasks, because most package modules accept a list in \u003ccode>name\u003c/code> and do it in one transaction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"handlers\">Handlers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>handler\u003c/strong> is a task that runs only when notified, and only once at the end of the play, however many tasks notified it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Web configuration\n  hosts: web\n  become: true\n  tasks:\n    - name: Render nginx configuration\n      ansible.builtin.template:\n        src: shop.conf.j2\n        dest: /etc/nginx/conf.d/shop.conf\n        validate: &quot;nginx -t -c /etc/nginx/nginx.conf&quot;\n      notify: Reload nginx\n\n    - name: Ensure nginx is running\n      ansible.builtin.service:\n        name: nginx\n        state: started\n\n  handlers:\n    - name: Reload nginx\n      ansible.builtin.service:\n        name: nginx\n        state: reloaded\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Web\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">configuration\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">become:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tasks:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Render\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">configuration\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.template:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">src:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop.conf.j2\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">dest:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/etc/nginx/conf.d/shop.conf\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">validate:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;nginx -t -c /etc/nginx/nginx.conf&quot;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">notify:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Reload\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ensure\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">is\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">running\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.service:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">state:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">started\u003c/span>\n\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">handlers:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Reload\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.service:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">state:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reloaded\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The reload happens only if the template task reported \u003ccode>changed\u003c/code>. If a later task fails before the handlers run, the handler is skipped unless you use \u003ccode>meta: flush_handlers\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>--force-handlers\u003c/code>, which is a common source of &quot;config changed but service not reloaded&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"handling-errors\">Handling errors\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Deploy with a safety net\n  block:\n    - name: Update the application\n      ansible.builtin.copy:\n        src: app-v2.tar.gz\n        dest: /opt/shop/app.tar.gz\n    - name: Restart the application\n      ansible.builtin.service:\n        name: shop\n        state: restarted\n  rescue:\n    - name: Roll back to the previous release\n      ansible.builtin.command: /opt/shop/bin/rollback\n  always:\n    - name: Report the outcome\n      ansible.builtin.debug:\n        msg: &quot;Deployment attempt finished&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Deploy\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">with\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">a\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">safety\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">net\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">block:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Update\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">application\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.copy:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">src:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app-v2.tar.gz\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">dest:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/opt/shop/app.tar.gz\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Restart\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">application\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.service:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">state:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">restarted\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rescue:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Roll\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">back\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">to\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">previous\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">release\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.command:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/opt/shop/bin/rollback\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">always:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Report\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">outcome\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.debug:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">msg:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Deployment attempt finished&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>rescue\u003c/code> runs if something in the block fails, and \u003ccode>always\u003c/code> runs regardless. \u003ccode>ignore_errors: true\u003c/code> is different: it keeps going after a failure and hides the problem, so use it rarely and with a comment.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tags-and-limiting\">Tags and limiting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-playbook site.yml --tags config\nansible-playbook site.yml --skip-tags slow\nansible-playbook site.yml --list-tasks\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ansible-playbook site.yml --tags config\nansible-playbook site.yml --skip-tags slow\nansible-playbook site.yml --list-tasks\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Tags let you run part of a playbook. Combine with \u003ccode>--limit\u003c/code> to test on one host before the fleet.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Three tasks all notify &quot;Reload nginx&quot; and all report changed. How many times does the handler run?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Once, at the end of the play. Handlers are deduplicated, and they run only if at least one notifying task changed something.\u003c/p>\u003cp>That behaviour avoids repeated restarts during one run.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"task&gt;notify;notify&gt;end;end&gt;handler\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How a change triggers a reload\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"task\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Config task\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"notify\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Notify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"end\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">End of play\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"handler\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Handler runs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Config task to Notify; 2, Notify to End of play; 3, End of play to Handler runs.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"task\">\u003cdt>Config task \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Renders the file and reports changed or ok.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An always-changed task triggers needless reloads.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"notify\">\u003cdt>Notify \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Queues the handler only if the task changed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A typo in the handler name means it never runs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"end\">\u003cdt>End of play \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Queued handlers run once each after all tasks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A failure before this point may skip the handler.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"handler\">\u003cdt>Handler runs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reloads the service with the new configuration.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A reload without validation can take the service down.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-handler-that-never-ran\">Incident: the handler that never ran\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new certificate was deployed and the play reported success, but the site kept serving the old one for days because the play failed after the copy and before the handlers.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-playbook site.yml\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-playbook site.yml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">TASK [Copy certificate] changed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">TASK [Run database check] fatal: FAILED\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">RUNNING HANDLER [Reload nginx] skipped\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team added \u003ccode>meta: flush_handlers\u003c/code> right after the copy task and turned on \u003ccode>--force-handlers\u003c/code> in the pipeline. They also added a post-deploy check that reads the certificate expiry date from the live site.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Braces inside \u003ccode>when\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>ignore_errors\u003c/code> instead of fixing or rescuing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restarting a service from a normal task (it restarts on every run)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting everything in one giant play with no tags\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"conditions","text":"Conditions"},{"id":"loops","text":"Loops"},{"id":"handlers","text":"Handlers"},{"id":"handling-errors","text":"Handling errors"},{"id":"tags-and-limiting","text":"Tags and limiting"},{"id":"incident-the-handler-that-never-ran","text":"Incident: the handler that never ran"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Handlers","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_handlers.html"},{"t":"Conditionals","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_conditionals.html"},{"t":"Loops","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_loops.html"},{"t":"Blocks and error handling","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_error_handling.html"}],"text":"Real servers differ and real changes need follow-up: reload the web server only if its config changed, install a package only on Debian family hosts, create five users without five copied tasks. These tools give a playbook that flow without turning it into a script. What you will learn when conditions loop over lists and dictionaries Handlers and when they run block , rescue and always Tags and limiting a run Conditions - name: Install on Debian family ansible.builtin.apt: name: nginx state: present when: ansible_facts[&#x27;os_family&#x27;] == \"Debian\" - name: Install on Red Hat family ansible.builtin.dnf: name: nginx state: present when: ansible_facts[&#x27;os_family&#x27;] == \"RedHat\" when takes a plain expression with no braces. Combine conditions with and , or and not , and use is defined to test a variable. Loops - name: Create service users ansible.builtin.user: name: \" {{ item.name }} \" groups: \" {{ item.groups }} \" state: present loop: - { name: alice , groups: \"sudo\" } - { name: bob , groups: \"developers\" } Use loop_control with label to keep output short when items are large. Do not loop over a package list with many tasks, because most package modules accept a list in name and do it in one transaction. Handlers A handler is a task that runs only when notified, and only once at the end of the play, however many tasks notified it. - name: Web configuration hosts: web become: true tasks: - name: Render nginx configuration ansible.builtin.template: src: shop.conf.j2 dest: /etc/nginx/conf.d/shop.conf validate: \"nginx -t -c /etc/nginx/nginx.conf\" notify: Reload nginx - name: Ensure nginx is running ansible.builtin.service: name: nginx state: started handlers: - name: Reload nginx ansible.builtin.service: name: nginx state: reloaded The reload happens only if the template task reported changed . If a later task fails before the handlers run, the handler is skipped unless you use meta: flush_handlers or --force-handlers , which is a common source of \"config changed but service not reloaded\". Handling errors - name: Deploy with a safety net block: - name: Update the application ansible.builtin.copy: src: app-v2.tar.gz dest: /opt/shop/app.tar.gz - name: Restart the application ansible.builtin.service: name: shop state: restarted rescue: - name: Roll back to the previous release ansible.builtin.command: /opt/shop/bin/rollback always: - name: Report the outcome ansible.builtin.debug: msg: \"Deployment attempt finished\" rescue runs if something in the block fails, and always runs regardless. ignore_errors: true is different: it keeps going after a failure and hides the problem, so use it rarely and with a comment. Tags and limiting ansible-playbook site.yml --tags config ansible-playbook site.yml --skip-tags slow ansible-playbook site.yml --list-tasks Tags let you run part of a playbook. Combine with --limit to test on one host before the fleet. Predict first. Three tasks all notify \"Reload nginx\" and all report changed. How many times does the handler run? Your guess Answer: Once, at the end of the play. Handlers are deduplicated, and they run only if at least one notifying task changed something. That behaviour avoids repeated restarts during one run. How a change triggers a reload Flow in order: 1, Config task to Notify; 2, Notify to End of play; 3, End of play to Handler runs. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Config task Step What it does: Renders the file and reports changed or ok. If it fails or is weak: An always-changed task triggers needless reloads. Notify Step What it does: Queues the handler only if the task changed. If it fails or is weak: A typo in the handler name means it never runs. End of play Step What it does: Queued handlers run once each after all tasks. If it fails or is weak: A failure before this point may skip the handler. Handler runs Step What it does: Reloads the service with the new configuration. If it fails or is"},"ansible/roles-and-collections":{"html":"\u003cp>A single playbook grows until nobody wants to touch it. Roles package the tasks, templates, defaults and handlers for one job (for example &quot;nginx&quot; or &quot;postgres&quot;) so that many playbooks can reuse them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The standard role directory layout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Role defaults versus vars\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using roles in a play, and passing variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Collections and \u003ccode>requirements.yml\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"role-layout\">Role layout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"roles/\n  nginx/\n    defaults/main.yml     # lowest-precedence defaults, meant to be overridden\n    vars/main.yml         # high-precedence internal constants\n    tasks/main.yml        # the work\n    handlers/main.yml     # reload and restart handlers\n    templates/site.conf.j2\n    files/\n    meta/main.yml         # dependencies and metadata\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">roles/\n  nginx/\n    defaults/main.yml     # lowest-precedence defaults, meant to be overridden\n    vars/main.yml         # high-precedence internal constants\n    tasks/main.yml        # the work\n    handlers/main.yml     # reload and restart handlers\n    templates/site.conf.j2\n    files/\n    meta/main.yml         # dependencies and metadata\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Create the skeleton with \u003ccode>ansible-galaxy init roles/nginx\u003c/code>. Only \u003ccode>tasks/main.yml\u003c/code> is required.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-small-role\">A small role\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# roles/nginx/defaults/main.yml\nnginx_port: 80\nnginx_site_name: example.com\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># roles/nginx/defaults/main.yml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">nginx_port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">nginx_site_name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">example.com\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# roles/nginx/tasks/main.yml\n- name: Install nginx\n  ansible.builtin.apt:\n    name: nginx\n    state: present\n\n- name: Render site configuration\n  ansible.builtin.template:\n    src: site.conf.j2\n    dest: /etc/nginx/conf.d/site.conf\n  notify: Reload nginx\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># roles/nginx/tasks/main.yml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Install\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.apt:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">state:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">present\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Render\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">site\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">configuration\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">src:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">site.conf.j2\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">dest:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/etc/nginx/conf.d/site.conf\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">notify:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Reload\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# roles/nginx/handlers/main.yml\n- name: Reload nginx\n  ansible.builtin.service:\n    name: nginx\n    state: reloaded\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># roles/nginx/handlers/main.yml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Reload\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.service:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">state:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reloaded\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use it from a playbook:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Web tier\n  hosts: web\n  become: true\n  roles:\n    - role: nginx\n      vars:\n        nginx_port: 8080\n        nginx_site_name: shop.example.com\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Web\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tier\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">become:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">roles:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">role:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">vars:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">nginx_port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">nginx_site_name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop.example.com\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"design-rules-for-roles\">Design rules for roles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>One job per role\u003c/strong>, with a clear name and a short README\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Prefix every variable\u003c/strong> with the role name (\u003ccode>nginx_port\u003c/code>) so roles do not collide\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Put overridable settings in \u003ccode>defaults\u003c/code>, and keep \u003ccode>vars\u003c/code> for values users should not change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Avoid hidden dependencies on other roles&#39; variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep roles free of environment-specific values; those belong in inventory\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"collections\">Collections\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>collection\u003c/strong> bundles modules, plugins and roles for a product or cloud, such as \u003ccode>amazon.aws\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>community.postgresql\u003c/code>. Install the ones you need and pin versions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# requirements.yml\ncollections:\n  - name: amazon.aws\n    version: 8.2.1\n  - name: community.general\n    version: 9.5.0\nroles:\n  - name: geerlingguy.nginx\n    version: 3.2.0\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># requirements.yml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">collections:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">amazon.aws\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.1\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">community.general\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">9.5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">roles:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">geerlingguy.nginx\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-galaxy install -r requirements.yml\nansible-galaxy collection list\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ansible-galaxy install -r requirements.yml\nansible-galaxy collection list\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Pinning matters. An unpinned role or collection can change under you and make last month&#39;s playbook behave differently. Review third-party roles before using them in production, because they run with your privileges.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Role defaults set nginx_port to 80. The play passes nginx_port: 8080 in the role's vars. Which value is used?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> 8080. Defaults are the lowest precedence, so any value you pass overrides them.\u003c/p>\u003cp>That is the whole point of putting settings in defaults.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"play&gt;role;role&gt;coll;role&gt;host\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From playbook to configured server using roles\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"play\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Playbook\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"role\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Role\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"coll\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Collection\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"host\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Managed node\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Playbook to Role; 2, Role to Collection; 3, Role to Managed node.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"play\">\u003cdt>Playbook \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Lists hosts and the roles to apply.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A play with no pinned role versions can change between runs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"role\">\u003cdt>Role \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tasks, templates, handlers and defaults for one job.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Variables without a prefix collide with other roles.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"coll\">\u003cdt>Collection \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Supplies modules and plugins that the role calls.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An unpinned collection can change behaviour after an update.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"host\">\u003cdt>Managed node \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Receives the rendered files and service changes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A role that assumes one OS breaks on another.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-role-update-that-broke-a-deploy\">Incident: the role update that broke a deploy\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An unpinned third-party role was updated upstream and changed a default port. The next scheduled run reconfigured production web servers on the wrong port.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-galaxy role list\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-galaxy role list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">- geerlingguy.nginx, (unknown version)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>After the outage the team pinned every role and collection in \u003ccode>requirements.yml\u003c/code>, mirrored the ones they depend on internally, and updated them on purpose in a pull request that runs the tests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Giant roles that install five unrelated things\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unprefixed variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using unpinned roles from the internet\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting production hostnames inside roles\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"role-layout","text":"Role layout"},{"id":"a-small-role","text":"A small role"},{"id":"design-rules-for-roles","text":"Design rules for roles"},{"id":"collections","text":"Collections"},{"id":"incident-the-role-update-that-broke-a-deploy","text":"Incident: the role update that broke a deploy"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Roles","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_reuse_roles.html"},{"t":"Using collections","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/collections_guide/index.html"},{"t":"Galaxy user guide","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/galaxy/user_guide.html"}],"text":"A single playbook grows until nobody wants to touch it. Roles package the tasks, templates, defaults and handlers for one job (for example \"nginx\" or \"postgres\") so that many playbooks can reuse them. What you will learn The standard role directory layout Role defaults versus vars Using roles in a play, and passing variables Collections and requirements.yml Role layout roles/ nginx/ defaults/main.yml # lowest-precedence defaults, meant to be overridden vars/main.yml # high-precedence internal constants tasks/main.yml # the work handlers/main.yml # reload and restart handlers templates/site.conf.j2 files/ meta/main.yml # dependencies and metadata Create the skeleton with ansible-galaxy init roles/nginx . Only tasks/main.yml is required. A small role # roles/nginx/defaults/main.yml nginx_port: 80 nginx_site_name: example.com # roles/nginx/tasks/main.yml - name: Install nginx ansible.builtin.apt: name: nginx state: present - name: Render site configuration ansible.builtin.template: src: site.conf.j2 dest: /etc/nginx/conf.d/site.conf notify: Reload nginx # roles/nginx/handlers/main.yml - name: Reload nginx ansible.builtin.service: name: nginx state: reloaded Use it from a playbook: - name: Web tier hosts: web become: true roles: - role: nginx vars: nginx_port: 8080 nginx_site_name: shop.example.com Design rules for roles One job per role , with a clear name and a short README Prefix every variable with the role name ( nginx_port ) so roles do not collide Put overridable settings in defaults , and keep vars for values users should not change Avoid hidden dependencies on other roles&#39; variables Keep roles free of environment-specific values; those belong in inventory Collections A collection bundles modules, plugins and roles for a product or cloud, such as amazon.aws or community.postgresql . Install the ones you need and pin versions. # requirements.yml collections: - name: amazon.aws version: 8.2 .1 - name: community.general version: 9.5 .0 roles: - name: geerlingguy.nginx version: 3.2 .0 ansible-galaxy install -r requirements.yml ansible-galaxy collection list Pinning matters. An unpinned role or collection can change under you and make last month&#39;s playbook behave differently. Review third-party roles before using them in production, because they run with your privileges. Predict first. Role defaults set nginx_port to 80. The play passes nginx_port: 8080 in the role's vars. Which value is used? Your guess Answer: 8080. Defaults are the lowest precedence, so any value you pass overrides them. That is the whole point of putting settings in defaults. From playbook to configured server using roles Flow in order: 1, Playbook to Role; 2, Role to Collection; 3, Role to Managed node. Step Security Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Playbook Step What it does: Lists hosts and the roles to apply. If it fails or is weak: A play with no pinned role versions can change between runs. Role Security What it does: Tasks, templates, handlers and defaults for one job. If it fails or is weak: Variables without a prefix collide with other roles. Collection Step What it does: Supplies modules and plugins that the role calls. If it fails or is weak: An unpinned collection can change behaviour after an update. Managed node Service What it does: Receives the rendered files and service changes. If it fails or is weak: A role that assumes one OS breaks on another. Incident: the role update that broke a deploy Incident An unpinned third-party role was updated upstream and changed a default port. The next scheduled run reconfigured production web servers on the wrong port. $ ansible-galaxy role list - geerlingguy.nginx, (unknown version) After the outage the team pinned every role and collection in requirements.yml , mirrored the ones they depend on internally, and updated them on purpose in a pull request that runs the tests. Common mistakes Giant roles that inst"},"ansible/vault-secrets-and-safe-practice":{"html":"\u003cp>Automation holds the keys to your servers: SSH access, sudo rights, database passwords and API tokens. If those leak, the blast radius is every machine the automation can reach, so secret handling is part of the job, not an extra.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Encrypting values and files with Ansible Vault\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping secrets out of logs with \u003ccode>no_log\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using an external secret manager instead\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Least privilege for the automation itself\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ansible-vault\">Ansible Vault\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Vault encrypts a file or a single value so it can sit next to your playbooks. Without the vault password it is unreadable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-vault create group_vars/prod/vault.yml\nansible-vault edit group_vars/prod/vault.yml\nansible-vault encrypt_string 's3cret-value' --name 'vault_db_password'\nansible-playbook site.yml --vault-id prod@prompt\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ansible-vault create group_vars/prod/vault.yml\nansible-vault edit group_vars/prod/vault.yml\nansible-vault encrypt_string \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;s3cret-value&#x27;\u003c/span> --name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;vault_db_password&#x27;\u003c/span>\nansible-playbook site.yml --vault-id prod@prompt\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A common pattern keeps readable variable names pointing to encrypted ones:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# group_vars/prod/vars.yml (readable, safe to review)\ndb_password: &quot;{{ vault_db_password }}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># group_vars/prod/vars.yml (readable, safe to review)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">db_password:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ vault_db_password }}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# group_vars/prod/vault.yml (encrypted with ansible-vault)\nvault_db_password: placeholder-replaced-by-encrypted-content\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># group_vars/prod/vault.yml (encrypted with ansible-vault)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">vault_db_password:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">placeholder-replaced-by-encrypted-content\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Reviewers can see which variables exist and where they are used, while the values stay encrypted. Use separate vault IDs for different environments so a developer with staging access cannot read production secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-the-vault-password-comes-from\">Where the vault password comes from\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Prompt interactively (\u003ccode>--vault-id prod@prompt\u003c/code>)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A password file outside the repository with tight permissions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A script that fetches it from your secret manager or CI secret store\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Never commit the vault password next to the encrypted files. That defeats the point.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-secrets-out-of-output\">Keep secrets out of output\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Verbose output and logs can print task arguments. Mark sensitive tasks:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Set the database password\n  community.postgresql.postgresql_user:\n    name: shop\n    password: &quot;{{ db_password }}&quot;\n  no_log: true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Set\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">database\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">password\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">community.postgresql.postgresql_user:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">password:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ db_password }}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">no_log:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>no_log: true\u003c/code> hides the task&#39;s arguments and results, including useful error details, so enable it only for tasks that handle secrets and debug with a safe test copy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"better-do-not-store-long-lived-secrets-at-all\">Better: do not store long-lived secrets at all\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Vault is fine for small teams. Larger teams often fetch secrets at run time from a manager such as HashiCorp Vault or a cloud secret service using a lookup plugin, so secrets are rotated and audited in one place. Whatever you choose, rotate any secret that has ever appeared in a log or a repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"least-privilege-for-the-automation\">Least privilege for the automation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use a dedicated SSH key or short-lived certificate for Ansible, not a person&#39;s key\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Give the account only the sudo commands it needs where possible\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Limit which hosts the control node can reach with network rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protect the control node like production: patch it, restrict logins, log access\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Review third-party roles and collections before running them as root\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A task prints the database password in the output when you run with -vvv. Which setting prevents this?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> no_log: true on that task. It hides the arguments and result from the console and logs.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Remember to remove it temporarily on a test copy if you need the hidden error text.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"store&gt;play;pw&gt;play;play&gt;host\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How a secret travels through a run\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"store\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Encrypted vault file\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pw\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Vault password source\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"play\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Playbook run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"host\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Target host\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Encrypted vault file to Playbook run; 2, Vault password source to Playbook run; 3, Playbook run to Target host.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"store\">\u003cdt>Encrypted vault file \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The value is stored encrypted in the repository.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A leaked vault password exposes every value in the file.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pw\">\u003cdt>Vault password source \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Prompt, protected file or secret manager.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A password committed with the files cancels the encryption.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"play\">\u003cdt>Playbook run \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Decrypts in memory and renders tasks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Verbose output may print values without no_log.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"host\">\u003cdt>Target host \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The secret is written to a protected file or passed to a service.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A world-readable file undoes the protection.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-password-in-the-build-log\">Incident: the password in the build log\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A database password appeared in a CI build log because a task ran with verbose output and no \u003ccode>no_log\u003c/code>. The log was readable by the whole company.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-playbook site.yml -vvv | grep -i password\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-playbook site.yml -vvv | grep -i password\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;password&quot;: &quot;REDACTED-IN-THIS-EXAMPLE&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team rotated the password, added \u003ccode>no_log\u003c/code> to every task that handles credentials, restricted who can read pipeline logs, and added a scanner that fails the pipeline if secrets appear in output.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Committing the vault password file\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One vault password for all environments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>no_log\u003c/code> everywhere so failures become undebuggable\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running automation with a personal, over-privileged account\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"ansible-vault","text":"Ansible Vault"},{"id":"where-the-vault-password-comes-from","text":"Where the vault password comes from"},{"id":"keep-secrets-out-of-output","text":"Keep secrets out of output"},{"id":"better-do-not-store-long-lived-secrets-at-all","text":"Better: do not store long-lived secrets at all"},{"id":"least-privilege-for-the-automation","text":"Least privilege for the automation"},{"id":"incident-the-password-in-the-build-log","text":"Incident: the password in the build log"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Protecting sensitive data with Ansible Vault","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/vault_guide/index.html"},{"t":"Keeping secrets out of logs (no_log)","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/reference_appendices/logging.html"},{"t":"Privilege escalation (become)","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_privilege_escalation.html"}],"text":"Automation holds the keys to your servers: SSH access, sudo rights, database passwords and API tokens. If those leak, the blast radius is every machine the automation can reach, so secret handling is part of the job, not an extra. What you will learn Encrypting values and files with Ansible Vault Keeping secrets out of logs with no_log Using an external secret manager instead Least privilege for the automation itself Ansible Vault Vault encrypts a file or a single value so it can sit next to your playbooks. Without the vault password it is unreadable. ansible-vault create group_vars/prod/vault.yml ansible-vault edit group_vars/prod/vault.yml ansible-vault encrypt_string &#x27;s3cret-value&#x27; --name &#x27;vault_db_password&#x27; ansible-playbook site.yml --vault-id prod@prompt A common pattern keeps readable variable names pointing to encrypted ones: # group_vars/prod/vars.yml (readable, safe to review) db_password: \" {{ vault_db_password }} \" # group_vars/prod/vault.yml (encrypted with ansible-vault) vault_db_password: placeholder-replaced-by-encrypted-content Reviewers can see which variables exist and where they are used, while the values stay encrypted. Use separate vault IDs for different environments so a developer with staging access cannot read production secrets. Where the vault password comes from Prompt interactively ( --vault-id prod@prompt ) A password file outside the repository with tight permissions A script that fetches it from your secret manager or CI secret store Never commit the vault password next to the encrypted files. That defeats the point. Keep secrets out of output Verbose output and logs can print task arguments. Mark sensitive tasks: - name: Set the database password community.postgresql.postgresql_user: name: shop password: \" {{ db_password }} \" no_log: true no_log: true hides the task&#39;s arguments and results, including useful error details, so enable it only for tasks that handle secrets and debug with a safe test copy. Better: do not store long-lived secrets at all Vault is fine for small teams. Larger teams often fetch secrets at run time from a manager such as HashiCorp Vault or a cloud secret service using a lookup plugin, so secrets are rotated and audited in one place. Whatever you choose, rotate any secret that has ever appeared in a log or a repository. Least privilege for the automation Use a dedicated SSH key or short-lived certificate for Ansible, not a person&#39;s key Give the account only the sudo commands it needs where possible Limit which hosts the control node can reach with network rules Protect the control node like production: patch it, restrict logins, log access Review third-party roles and collections before running them as root Predict first. A task prints the database password in the output when you run with -vvv. Which setting prevents this? Your guess Answer: no_log: true on that task. It hides the arguments and result from the console and logs. Remember to remove it temporarily on a test copy if you need the hidden error text. How a secret travels through a run Flow in order: 1, Encrypted vault file to Playbook run; 2, Vault password source to Playbook run; 3, Playbook run to Target host. Security Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Encrypted vault file Security What it does: The value is stored encrypted in the repository. If it fails or is weak: A leaked vault password exposes every value in the file. Vault password source Security What it does: Prompt, protected file or secret manager. If it fails or is weak: A password committed with the files cancels the encryption. Playbook run Step What it does: Decrypts in memory and renders tasks. If it fails or is weak: Verbose output may print values without no_log. Target host Service What it does: The secret is written to a protected file or passed to a service. If it fails or is weak: A world-readable file undoes the protect"},"ansible/ansible-at-scale":{"html":"\u003cp>Running a playbook against three servers is easy. Running it against three hundred during business hours means batches, health checks, a way to stop, and tests that run before the change reaches anyone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rolling updates with \u003ccode>serial\u003c/code> and failure limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Linting and testing roles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Performance settings for large inventories\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where Ansible stops and Terraform starts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A controller such as AWX or a pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rolling-updates\">Rolling updates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Rolling update of the web tier\n  hosts: web\n  become: true\n  serial: &quot;20%&quot;\n  max_fail_percentage: 10\n  tasks:\n    - name: Take the host out of the load balancer\n      ansible.builtin.command: /opt/tools/lb-drain {{ inventory_hostname }}\n      delegate_to: localhost\n    - name: Update the application\n      ansible.builtin.apt:\n        name: shop-app\n        state: latest\n    - name: Wait for the health endpoint\n      ansible.builtin.uri:\n        url: &quot;http://localhost:8080/health&quot;\n        status_code: 200\n      register: health\n      retries: 10\n      delay: 5\n      until: health.status == 200\n    - name: Put the host back\n      ansible.builtin.command: /opt/tools/lb-enable {{ inventory_hostname }}\n      delegate_to: localhost\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Rolling\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">update\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">of\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tier\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">become:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">serial:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;20%&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">max_fail_percentage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tasks:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Take\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">host\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">out\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">of\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">load\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">balancer\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.command:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/opt/tools/lb-drain\u003c/span> {{ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">inventory_hostname\u003c/span> }}\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">delegate_to:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">localhost\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Update\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">application\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.apt:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-app\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">state:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">latest\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Wait\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">for\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">health\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">endpoint\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.uri:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">url:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;http://localhost:8080/health&quot;\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">status_code:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">200\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">register:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">health\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">retries:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">delay:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">until:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">health.status\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">==\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">200\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Put\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">host\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">back\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.command:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/opt/tools/lb-enable\u003c/span> {{ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">inventory_hostname\u003c/span> }}\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">delegate_to:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">localhost\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>serial\u003c/code> updates a batch at a time instead of everything at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>max_fail_percentage\u003c/code> aborts the play when too many hosts fail\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>retries\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>until\u003c/code> waits for readiness rather than guessing a sleep\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>delegate_to\u003c/code> runs a task on another machine such as a load balancer or localhost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>any_errors_fatal: true\u003c/code> stops the whole play at the first failure when a partial rollout is worse\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-before-you-roll\">Test before you roll\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-lint site.yml\nyamllint .\nansible-playbook site.yml --syntax-check\nansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff --limit web1.example.com\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ansible-lint site.yml\nyamllint .\nansible-playbook site.yml --syntax-check\nansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">limit\u003c/span> web1.example.com\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For roles, \u003cstrong>Molecule\u003c/strong> creates a throwaway container or VM, applies the role, runs it a second time to prove idempotence, then runs verification checks. A pipeline that runs lint, Molecule and a staging run catches most mistakes before production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"speed\">Speed\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Turn off \u003ccode>gather_facts\u003c/code> or use \u003ccode>gather_subset\u003c/code> when you do not need all facts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Raise \u003ccode>forks\u003c/code> (parallel connections, default 5) to match your control node and network\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enable SSH connection reuse (\u003ccode>ControlPersist\u003c/code>) and pipelining\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>strategy: free\u003c/code> only when hosts are independent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Avoid loops that call a module once per item when it accepts a list\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ansible-and-terraform\">Ansible and Terraform\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>They solve neighbouring problems and work well together.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Question\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Better fit\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Create networks, servers, databases, load balancers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Terraform\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Install packages, render configuration, manage users on a server\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Ansible\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Keep a machine matching a description over time\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Ansible, or rebuild from an image\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Build the server image once\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A tool such as Packer, then Ansible inside it\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A common flow: Terraform creates instances and outputs addresses, a dynamic inventory reads them, and Ansible configures them. For container and Kubernetes workloads, immutable images often replace much of host configuration, but Ansible still manages the hosts that run them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"running-it-for-a-team\">Running it for a team\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Running playbooks from laptops makes results depend on who ran them. A shared controller (AWX or the supported Automation Platform) or a CI pipeline gives you credentials kept in one place, job history, role-based access, scheduling and a standard environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A play with serial: 2 targets ten hosts and the third host in the first batch fails. What happens to the remaining hosts?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Hosts already processed stay changed. Whether the remaining batches run depends on settings: by default the play continues for hosts that have not failed, but max_fail_percentage or any_errors_fatal can stop it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Decide your stop rule before the rollout, not during it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"lint&gt;canary;canary&gt;waves;waves&gt;verify\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A safe fleet rollout\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lint\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Lint and test\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"canary\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Canary batch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"waves\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Serial waves\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"verify\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Lint and test to Canary batch; 2, Canary batch to Serial waves; 3, Serial waves to Verify.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lint\">\u003cdt>Lint and test \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> ansible-lint, Molecule and check mode on a test host.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping tests sends mistakes to every server at once.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"canary\">\u003cdt>Canary batch \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Update one small batch first and verify health.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A canary without a health check proves nothing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"waves\">\u003cdt>Serial waves \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Continue in batches, draining and restoring each host.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without a failure limit one bad change spreads fleet-wide.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"verify\">\u003cdt>Verify \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Check version, health and error rates after each wave.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No verification means failures are found by users.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-update-that-went-to-every-server-at-once\">Incident: the update that went to every server at once\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A playbook with no \u003ccode>serial\u003c/code> upgraded a library on all web servers simultaneously. The new version had a startup bug, and every server was down at the same moment.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-playbook upgrade.yml --list-hosts | tail -1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-playbook upgrade.yml --list-hosts | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  hosts (240):\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team added \u003ccode>serial\u003c/code> with a canary batch, a health check between waves and \u003ccode>max_fail_percentage\u003c/code>, and made the pipeline refuse to run a production playbook that has none of these.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running all hosts at once during business hours\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using fixed sleeps instead of health checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using Ansible to create and destroy cloud infrastructure that Terraform already owns\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting laptops run production changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"rolling-updates","text":"Rolling updates"},{"id":"test-before-you-roll","text":"Test before you roll"},{"id":"speed","text":"Speed"},{"id":"ansible-and-terraform","text":"Ansible and Terraform"},{"id":"running-it-for-a-team","text":"Running it for a team"},{"id":"incident-the-update-that-went-to-every-server-at-once","text":"Incident: the update that went to every server at once"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Controlling playbook execution: strategies and batches","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_strategies.html"},{"t":"Ansible Lint","u":"https://ansible.readthedocs.io/projects/lint/"},{"t":"Molecule","u":"https://ansible.readthedocs.io/projects/molecule/"}],"text":"Running a playbook against three servers is easy. Running it against three hundred during business hours means batches, health checks, a way to stop, and tests that run before the change reaches anyone. What you will learn Rolling updates with serial and failure limits Linting and testing roles Performance settings for large inventories Where Ansible stops and Terraform starts A controller such as AWX or a pipeline Rolling updates - name: Rolling update of the web tier hosts: web become: true serial: \"20%\" max_fail_percentage: 10 tasks: - name: Take the host out of the load balancer ansible.builtin.command: /opt/tools/lb-drain {{ inventory_hostname }} delegate_to: localhost - name: Update the application ansible.builtin.apt: name: shop-app state: latest - name: Wait for the health endpoint ansible.builtin.uri: url: \"http://localhost:8080/health\" status_code: 200 register: health retries: 10 delay: 5 until: health.status == 200 - name: Put the host back ansible.builtin.command: /opt/tools/lb-enable {{ inventory_hostname }} delegate_to: localhost serial updates a batch at a time instead of everything at once max_fail_percentage aborts the play when too many hosts fail retries with until waits for readiness rather than guessing a sleep delegate_to runs a task on another machine such as a load balancer or localhost any_errors_fatal: true stops the whole play at the first failure when a partial rollout is worse Test before you roll ansible-lint site.yml yamllint . ansible-playbook site.yml --syntax-check ansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff -- limit web1.example.com For roles, Molecule creates a throwaway container or VM, applies the role, runs it a second time to prove idempotence, then runs verification checks. A pipeline that runs lint, Molecule and a staging run catches most mistakes before production. Speed Turn off gather_facts or use gather_subset when you do not need all facts Raise forks (parallel connections, default 5) to match your control node and network Enable SSH connection reuse ( ControlPersist ) and pipelining Use strategy: free only when hosts are independent Avoid loops that call a module once per item when it accepts a list Ansible and Terraform They solve neighbouring problems and work well together. Question Better fit Create networks, servers, databases, load balancers Terraform Install packages, render configuration, manage users on a server Ansible Keep a machine matching a description over time Ansible, or rebuild from an image Build the server image once A tool such as Packer, then Ansible inside it A common flow: Terraform creates instances and outputs addresses, a dynamic inventory reads them, and Ansible configures them. For container and Kubernetes workloads, immutable images often replace much of host configuration, but Ansible still manages the hosts that run them. Running it for a team Running playbooks from laptops makes results depend on who ran them. A shared controller (AWX or the supported Automation Platform) or a CI pipeline gives you credentials kept in one place, job history, role-based access, scheduling and a standard environment. Predict first. A play with serial: 2 targets ten hosts and the third host in the first batch fails. What happens to the remaining hosts? Your guess Answer: Hosts already processed stay changed. Whether the remaining batches run depends on settings: by default the play continues for hosts that have not failed, but max_fail_percentage or any_errors_fatal can stop it. Decide your stop rule before the rollout, not during it. A safe fleet rollout Flow in order: 1, Lint and test to Canary batch; 2, Canary batch to Serial waves; 3, Serial waves to Verify. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Lint and test Step What it does: ansible-lint, Molecule and check mode on a test host. If it fails or is weak: Skipping tests sends mistakes to every server at once. Canary batch Step What it d"},"ansible/dynamic-inventory-for-cloud":{"html":"\u003cp>Servers in the cloud appear and disappear all day through autoscaling. A static inventory file is out of date by lunch. A dynamic inventory asks the cloud which machines exist right now and groups them by tag, so playbooks always target the real fleet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Inventory plugins and how they query the cloud\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Grouping and variables from tags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Combining static and dynamic sources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Checking and troubleshooting the result\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inventory-plugins\">Inventory plugins\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An inventory plugin reads a small YAML file that tells Ansible where to look.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# inventory/aws_ec2.yml\nplugin: amazon.aws.aws_ec2\nregions:\n  - eu-west-1\nfilters:\n  tag:Managed: ansible\n  instance-state-name: running\nkeyed_groups:\n  - key: tags.Role\n    prefix: role\n  - key: tags.Env\n    prefix: env\nhostnames:\n  - private-ip-address\ncompose:\n  ansible_host: private_ip_address\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># inventory/aws_ec2.yml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">plugin:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">amazon.aws.aws_ec2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">regions:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">eu-west-1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">filters:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tag:Managed:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ansible\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">instance-state-name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">running\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">keyed_groups:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">key:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tags.Role\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">prefix:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">role\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">key:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tags.Env\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">prefix:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">env\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hostnames:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">private-ip-address\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">compose:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible_host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">private_ip_address\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The plugin calls the cloud API with your credentials, creates groups such as \u003ccode>role_web\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>env_prod\u003c/code>, and sets variables from instance details. Use roles or instance profiles for credentials, not keys in the file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"target-by-tag\">Target by tag\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Playbooks target the generated groups. Intersection of groups limits the scope:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- hosts: role_web:&amp;env_prod\n  serial: 2\n  roles:\n    - nginx\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">role_web:&amp;env_prod\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">serial:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">roles:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The tag is now the contract: if a team forgets the \u003ccode>Role\u003c/code> tag, the host is not configured. Make tags mandatory through your provisioning code. Combine this with \u003ccode>serial\u003c/code> so that a change touches a few hosts at a time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mix-with-static-inventory\">Mix with static inventory\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A directory can contain several sources, and Ansible merges them:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"inventory/\n  aws_ec2.yml\n  static.ini\n  group_vars/\n    all.yml\n    role_web.yml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">inventory/\n  aws_ec2.yml\n  static.ini\n  group_vars/\n    all.yml\n    role_web.yml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use static files for things the cloud does not know, such as network devices or an office server. Group variables in \u003ccode>group_vars/role_web.yml\u003c/code> apply to the dynamic group automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-what-it-found\">Check what it found\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-inventory -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml --graph\nansible-inventory -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml --host 10.0.3.21\nansible -i inventory/ role_web -m ping\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-inventory -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml --graph\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-inventory -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml --host 10.0.3.21\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible -i inventory/ role_web -m ping\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If groups are empty, check the filters, region and credentials first. The inventory cache can hide changes: set a short cache timeout, or disable it while testing. Remember that instances that are still booting may not be ready for SSH, so wait for them in the playbook.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A new web server was launched but a playbook for the web group does not touch it. What do you check?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Whether it has the correct Role tag, whether it matches the plugin filters, and whether the inventory cache is stale.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The inventory is built from tags and filters.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-server-that-nobody-patched\">Incident: The server that nobody patched\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A monthly patch run reported success, but a security scan later found 6 servers still unpatched.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-inventory -i hosts.ini --graph | grep -c web\naws ec2 describe-instances --filters Name=tag:Role,Values=web --query &quot;length(Reservations[].Instances[])&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-inventory -i hosts.ini --graph | grep -c web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ec2 describe-instances --filters Name=tag:Role,Values=web --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;length(Reservations[].Instances[])&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">18\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The hand-written inventory listed 12 web servers while the autoscaler had grown the group to 18. The team moved to a dynamic inventory keyed on tags, required the tags in the provisioning module, and added a pre-run check that compares the inventory count with the cloud count. Patch runs now cover the whole fleet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Static host lists for autoscaled fleets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Untagged or wrongly tagged hosts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stale inventory cache\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Credentials stored in the inventory file\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"inventory-plugins","text":"Inventory plugins"},{"id":"target-by-tag","text":"Target by tag"},{"id":"mix-with-static-inventory","text":"Mix with static inventory"},{"id":"check-what-it-found","text":"Check what it found"},{"id":"incident-the-server-that-nobody-patched","text":"Incident: The server that nobody patched"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Ansible: Working with dynamic inventory","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/inventory_guide/intro_dynamic_inventory.html"},{"t":"Ansible: Inventory plugins","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/plugins/inventory.html"}],"text":"Servers in the cloud appear and disappear all day through autoscaling. A static inventory file is out of date by lunch. A dynamic inventory asks the cloud which machines exist right now and groups them by tag, so playbooks always target the real fleet. What you will learn Inventory plugins and how they query the cloud Grouping and variables from tags Combining static and dynamic sources Checking and troubleshooting the result Inventory plugins An inventory plugin reads a small YAML file that tells Ansible where to look. # inventory/aws_ec2.yml plugin: amazon.aws.aws_ec2 regions: - eu-west-1 filters: tag:Managed: ansible instance-state-name: running keyed_groups: - key: tags.Role prefix: role - key: tags.Env prefix: env hostnames: - private-ip-address compose: ansible_host: private_ip_address The plugin calls the cloud API with your credentials, creates groups such as role_web and env_prod , and sets variables from instance details. Use roles or instance profiles for credentials, not keys in the file. Target by tag Playbooks target the generated groups. Intersection of groups limits the scope: - hosts: role_web:&env_prod serial: 2 roles: - nginx The tag is now the contract: if a team forgets the Role tag, the host is not configured. Make tags mandatory through your provisioning code. Combine this with serial so that a change touches a few hosts at a time. Mix with static inventory A directory can contain several sources, and Ansible merges them: inventory/ aws_ec2.yml static.ini group_vars/ all.yml role_web.yml Use static files for things the cloud does not know, such as network devices or an office server. Group variables in group_vars/role_web.yml apply to the dynamic group automatically. Check what it found $ ansible-inventory -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml --graph $ ansible-inventory -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml --host 10.0.3.21 $ ansible -i inventory/ role_web -m ping If groups are empty, check the filters, region and credentials first. The inventory cache can hide changes: set a short cache timeout, or disable it while testing. Remember that instances that are still booting may not be ready for SSH, so wait for them in the playbook. Predict first. A new web server was launched but a playbook for the web group does not touch it. What do you check? Your guess Answer: Whether it has the correct Role tag, whether it matches the plugin filters, and whether the inventory cache is stale. The inventory is built from tags and filters. Incident: The server that nobody patched Incident A monthly patch run reported success, but a security scan later found 6 servers still unpatched. $ ansible-inventory -i hosts.ini --graph | grep -c web 12 $ aws ec2 describe-instances --filters Name=tag:Role,Values=web --query \"length(Reservations[].Instances[])\" 18 The hand-written inventory listed 12 web servers while the autoscaler had grown the group to 18. The team moved to a dynamic inventory keyed on tags, required the tags in the provisioning module, and added a pre-run check that compares the inventory count with the cloud count. Patch runs now cover the whole fleet. Common mistakes Static host lists for autoscaled fleets Untagged or wrongly tagged hosts Stale inventory cache Credentials stored in the inventory file"},"ansible/testing-and-linting-ansible":{"html":"\u003cp>A one-character YAML mistake in a role reached production and restarted every web server at once. Infrastructure code is code, and it deserves the same safety net: lint in the editor, tests in the pipeline, and a rehearsal on disposable machines.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Static checks: syntax and ansible-lint\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check and diff mode\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Molecule: test roles on disposable instances\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Idempotence tests and a pipeline layout\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"static-checks\">Static checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-playbook site.yml --syntax-check\nansible-lint\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-playbook site.yml --syntax-check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-lint\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ansible-lint\u003c/code> flags risky patterns: shell where a module exists, missing \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, tasks that are never idempotent, deprecated syntax and unquoted file modes. Keep a \u003ccode>.ansible-lint\u003c/code> file with the rules you follow and fix the warnings rather than ignoring them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# .ansible-lint\nprofile: production\nexclude_paths:\n  - molecule/\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># .ansible-lint\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">profile:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">production\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">exclude_paths:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">molecule/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-and-diff-mode\">Check and diff mode\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff --limit role_web\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">limit\u003c/span> role_web\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Check mode predicts changes without making them, and diff mode shows the exact file changes. It is excellent for review, but not perfect: tasks that depend on earlier changes, or modules that cannot predict (for example \u003ccode>command\u003c/code>), may be skipped. Treat it as a preview, not a guarantee.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"molecule\">Molecule\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Molecule creates disposable containers or virtual machines, applies a role, verifies the result, and destroys them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# molecule/default/molecule.yml\ndriver:\n  name: docker\nplatforms:\n  - name: debian12\n    image: debian:12\n    pre_build_image: true\nprovisioner:\n  name: ansible\nverifier:\n  name: ansible\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># molecule/default/molecule.yml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">driver:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">docker\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">platforms:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">debian12\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">debian:12\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pre_build_image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">provisioner:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ansible\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">verifier:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ansible\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# molecule/default/verify.yml\n- hosts: all\n  tasks:\n    - name: Nginx answers\n      ansible.builtin.uri:\n        url: http://localhost\n        status_code: 200\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># molecule/default/verify.yml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">all\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tasks:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Nginx\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">answers\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.uri:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">url:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">http://localhost\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">status_code:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">200\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>molecule test\u003c/code> runs the sequence. Test the platforms you really use, for example two operating system versions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"idempotence-and-pipeline\">Idempotence and pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A good role changes something the first time and nothing the second. Molecule checks this: the second converge must report \u003ccode>changed=0\u003c/code>. A task that always reports changed (often a raw \u003ccode>command\u003c/code>) hides real changes and makes check mode useless; add \u003ccode>changed_when\u003c/code> or use a module.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A sensible pipeline: lint and syntax check on every commit, Molecule on pull requests, check and diff mode against staging before production, and a canary run with \u003ccode>serial: 1\u003c/code> first.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Molecule reports changed=3 on the second run of a role. What does it mean?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Three tasks are not idempotent; they change something every time and should be fixed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Repeated runs should converge to no changes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-task-that-always-changed\">Incident: The task that always changed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly Ansible run reported 14 changes every night, so nobody noticed when a real, unwanted change appeared.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-lint roles/base\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">PLAY RECAP\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-01 : ok=42 changed=14 unreachable=0 failed=0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-lint roles/base\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">roles/base/tasks/main.yml:31: no-changed-when: Commands should not change things if nothing needs doing\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Several tasks used shell commands that always reported changed. The team replaced them with proper modules or added changed_when conditions, then added Molecule idempotence tests to the pipeline. Nightly runs now report zero changes in the normal case, so a non-zero number is a genuine signal worth an alert.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ignoring lint warnings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting check mode as a guarantee\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Roles that are not idempotent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing only on one operating system version\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"static-checks","text":"Static checks"},{"id":"check-and-diff-mode","text":"Check and diff mode"},{"id":"molecule","text":"Molecule"},{"id":"idempotence-and-pipeline","text":"Idempotence and pipeline"},{"id":"incident-the-task-that-always-changed","text":"Incident: The task that always changed"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"ansible-lint documentation","u":"https://ansible.readthedocs.io/projects/lint/"},{"t":"Molecule documentation","u":"https://ansible.readthedocs.io/projects/molecule/"}],"text":"A one-character YAML mistake in a role reached production and restarted every web server at once. Infrastructure code is code, and it deserves the same safety net: lint in the editor, tests in the pipeline, and a rehearsal on disposable machines. What you will learn Static checks: syntax and ansible-lint Check and diff mode Molecule: test roles on disposable instances Idempotence tests and a pipeline layout Static checks $ ansible-playbook site.yml --syntax-check $ ansible-lint ansible-lint flags risky patterns: shell where a module exists, missing name , tasks that are never idempotent, deprecated syntax and unquoted file modes. Keep a .ansible-lint file with the rules you follow and fix the warnings rather than ignoring them. # .ansible-lint profile: production exclude_paths: - molecule/ Check and diff mode $ ansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff -- limit role_web Check mode predicts changes without making them, and diff mode shows the exact file changes. It is excellent for review, but not perfect: tasks that depend on earlier changes, or modules that cannot predict (for example command ), may be skipped. Treat it as a preview, not a guarantee. Molecule Molecule creates disposable containers or virtual machines, applies a role, verifies the result, and destroys them. # molecule/default/molecule.yml driver: name: docker platforms: - name: debian12 image: debian:12 pre_build_image: true provisioner: name: ansible verifier: name: ansible # molecule/default/verify.yml - hosts: all tasks: - name: Nginx answers ansible.builtin.uri: url: http://localhost status_code: 200 molecule test runs the sequence. Test the platforms you really use, for example two operating system versions. Idempotence and pipeline A good role changes something the first time and nothing the second. Molecule checks this: the second converge must report changed=0 . A task that always reports changed (often a raw command ) hides real changes and makes check mode useless; add changed_when or use a module. A sensible pipeline: lint and syntax check on every commit, Molecule on pull requests, check and diff mode against staging before production, and a canary run with serial: 1 first. Predict first. Molecule reports changed=3 on the second run of a role. What does it mean? Your guess Answer: Three tasks are not idempotent; they change something every time and should be fixed. Repeated runs should converge to no changes. Incident: The task that always changed Incident A nightly Ansible run reported 14 changes every night, so nobody noticed when a real, unwanted change appeared. PLAY RECAP web-01 : ok=42 changed=14 unreachable=0 failed=0 $ ansible-lint roles/base roles/base/tasks/main.yml:31: no-changed-when: Commands should not change things if nothing needs doing Several tasks used shell commands that always reported changed. The team replaced them with proper modules or added changed_when conditions, then added Molecule idempotence tests to the pipeline. Nightly runs now report zero changes in the normal case, so a non-zero number is a genuine signal worth an alert. Common mistakes Ignoring lint warnings Trusting check mode as a guarantee Roles that are not idempotent Testing only on one operating system version"},"ansible/debugging-ansible-runs":{"html":"\u003cp>Ansible failures usually fall into four groups: the connection did not work, privilege escalation did not work, the data (variables, templates) was wrong, or the module failed on the target. Naming the group tells you where to look.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading the failure message and the recap\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>-v\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>debug\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--step\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--start-at-task\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing connection, privilege and Python problems\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Finding the real value of a variable\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Finding slow tasks\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"four-groups-of-failure\">Four groups of failure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Group\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical message\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>First step\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Connection\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>UNREACHABLE! ... Permission denied (publickey)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ssh -vvv user@host\u003c/code> with the same key\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Privilege\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Missing sudo password\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Permission denied\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Check \u003ccode>become\u003c/code>, sudoers and the user\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Data\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>an error naming a missing variable, or a template error\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>debug\u003c/code> the variable, \u003ccode>ansible-inventory --host\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Module on target\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>FAILED! ... No package matching\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Read the module message, run it ad hoc with \u003ccode>-vvv\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verbosity-and-a-closer-look\">Verbosity and a closer look\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-playbook site.yml -v          # task results\nansible-playbook site.yml -vvv        # connection details\nansible web1.example.com -i inventory.ini -m setup | head\nansible-playbook site.yml --start-at-task &quot;Render nginx configuration&quot;\nansible-playbook site.yml --step      # confirm each task\nansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff --limit web1.example.com\nansible-config dump --only-changed\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ansible-playbook site.yml -v          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># task results\u003c/span>\nansible-playbook site.yml -vvv        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># connection details\u003c/span>\nansible web1.example.com -i inventory.ini -m setup | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\nansible-playbook site.yml --start-at-task \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Render nginx configuration&quot;\u003c/span>\nansible-playbook site.yml --step      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># confirm each task\u003c/span>\nansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">limit\u003c/span> web1.example.com\nansible-config dump --only-changed\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>-vvv\u003c/code> shows the SSH command, which you can run by hand. \u003ccode>--step\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--start-at-task\u003c/code> help when a long play fails near the end and you do not want to repeat the first twenty tasks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"look-at-the-data\">Look at the data\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Show what the host really sees\n  ansible.builtin.debug:\n    var: db_host\n\n- name: Show a computed value\n  ansible.builtin.debug:\n    msg: &quot;{{ app_backends | length }} backends, first is {{ app_backends | first }}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Show\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">what\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">host\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">really\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sees\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.debug:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">var:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db_host\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Show\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">a\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">computed\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">value\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.debug:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">msg:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ app_backends | length }}\u003c/span> backends, first is \u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ app_backends | first }}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --host web1.example.com\nansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --list --yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --host web1.example.com\nansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --list --yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ansible-inventory --host\u003c/code> shows variables from inventory sources, which is the quickest way to catch a wrong value from the wrong place.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"typical-fixes\">Typical fixes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Permission denied (publickey)\u003c/strong>: wrong user or key. Confirm with plain \u003ccode>ssh\u003c/code>, set \u003ccode>ansible_user\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>ansible_ssh_private_key_file\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Host key verification failed\u003c/strong>: add the host key properly rather than turning checking off for all hosts.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Python not found on target\u003c/strong>: install Python, or set \u003ccode>ansible_python_interpreter\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Missing sudo password\u003c/strong>: allow passwordless sudo for the automation user, or provide \u003ccode>--ask-become-pass\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Missing variable in a template\u003c/strong>: define it, or use \u003ccode>default\u003c/code>, and fail early with an \u003ccode>assert\u003c/code> task that explains what is missing.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Slow run\u003c/strong>: use the \u003ccode>profile_tasks\u003c/code> callback to list the slowest tasks, then reduce fact gathering or raise forks.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- name: Stop early if required data is missing\n  ansible.builtin.assert:\n    that:\n      - db_host is defined\n      - app_port | int &gt; 1024\n    fail_msg: &quot;db_host must be set and app_port must be above 1024&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Stop\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">early\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">required\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">data\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">is\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">missing\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.assert:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">that:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db_host\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">is\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">defined\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app_port\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">|\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">int\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&gt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1024\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">fail_msg:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db_host must be set and app_port must be above 1024&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Do not &quot;fix&quot; a failure by adding \u003ccode>ignore_errors\u003c/code>. That moves the problem somewhere harder to see.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A play fails with &quot;UNREACHABLE! ... Permission denied (publickey)&quot; for one host only. What is the fastest check?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Run ssh -vvv with the same user and key to that host. Compare ansible_user, the key path and the authorised key on that server with the working hosts.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Ansible uses ordinary SSH, so a failure there is an SSH failure first.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"read&gt;conn;read&gt;data;conn&gt;mod;data&gt;mod;mod&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Narrowing down a failing Ansible run\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"read\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Read the error\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"conn\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Connection and privilege\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"data\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Variables and templates\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mod\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Module on the target\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fix and re-run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Read the error to Connection and privilege; 2, Read the error to Variables and templates; 3, Connection and privilege to Module on the target; 4, Variables and templates to Module on the target; 5, Module on the target to Fix and re-run.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"read\">\u003cdt>Read the error \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Note the host, task name and message type.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping to retries wastes time and may repeat damage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"conn\">\u003cdt>Connection and privilege \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> ssh -vvv, become settings, sudoers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong user or key looks like a random failure.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"data\">\u003cdt>Variables and templates \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> debug, ansible-inventory --host, assert tasks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A value from the wrong file gives wrong but valid output.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mod\">\u003cdt>Module on the target \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run the module ad hoc with -vvv on one host.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing packages or Python surface only at run time.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Fix and re-run \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Change code or data, test on one host, then run the fleet.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Re-running everything without a canary repeats the failure widely.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-template-that-rendered-an-empty-list\">Incident: the template that rendered an empty list\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A firewall template used a variable that was empty on one group of servers. Ansible rendered a valid file with no allow rules, and the load balancer health checks started failing.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ansible-inventory -i inventory/production/hosts.ini --host app7 | grep allowed\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ansible-inventory -i inventory/production/hosts.ini --host app7 | grep allowed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    &quot;allowed_sources&quot;: [],\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A missing entry in \u003ccode>group_vars\u003c/code> for that group caused it. The team added an \u003ccode>assert\u003c/code> that the list is not empty, a \u003ccode>--check --diff\u003c/code> step in the pipeline, and a canary host before wider rollout.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running with \u003ccode>ignore_errors\u003c/code> to make the red go away\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging on all hosts instead of one with \u003ccode>--limit\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Turning off host key checking for everyone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not reading the line above the error\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"four-groups-of-failure","text":"Four groups of failure"},{"id":"verbosity-and-a-closer-look","text":"Verbosity and a closer look"},{"id":"look-at-the-data","text":"Look at the data"},{"id":"typical-fixes","text":"Typical fixes"},{"id":"incident-the-template-that-rendered-an-empty-list","text":"Incident: the template that rendered an empty list"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Debugging tasks","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_debugger.html"},{"t":"Ansible FAQ","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/reference_appendices/faq.html"},{"t":"Validating tasks: check mode and diff mode","u":"https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_checkmode.html"}],"text":"Ansible failures usually fall into four groups: the connection did not work, privilege escalation did not work, the data (variables, templates) was wrong, or the module failed on the target. Naming the group tells you where to look. What you will learn Reading the failure message and the recap Using -v , debug , --step and --start-at-task Fixing connection, privilege and Python problems Finding the real value of a variable Finding slow tasks Four groups of failure Group Typical message First step Connection UNREACHABLE! ... Permission denied (publickey) ssh -vvv user@host with the same key Privilege Missing sudo password , Permission denied Check become , sudoers and the user Data an error naming a missing variable, or a template error debug the variable, ansible-inventory --host Module on target FAILED! ... No package matching Read the module message, run it ad hoc with -vvv Verbosity and a closer look ansible-playbook site.yml -v # task results ansible-playbook site.yml -vvv # connection details ansible web1.example.com -i inventory.ini -m setup | head ansible-playbook site.yml --start-at-task \"Render nginx configuration\" ansible-playbook site.yml --step # confirm each task ansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff -- limit web1.example.com ansible-config dump --only-changed -vvv shows the SSH command, which you can run by hand. --step and --start-at-task help when a long play fails near the end and you do not want to repeat the first twenty tasks. Look at the data - name: Show what the host really sees ansible.builtin.debug: var: db_host - name: Show a computed value ansible.builtin.debug: msg: \" {{ app_backends | length }} backends, first is {{ app_backends | first }} \" ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --host web1.example.com ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --list --yaml ansible-inventory --host shows variables from inventory sources, which is the quickest way to catch a wrong value from the wrong place. Typical fixes Permission denied (publickey) : wrong user or key. Confirm with plain ssh , set ansible_user and ansible_ssh_private_key_file . Host key verification failed : add the host key properly rather than turning checking off for all hosts. Python not found on target : install Python, or set ansible_python_interpreter . Missing sudo password : allow passwordless sudo for the automation user, or provide --ask-become-pass . Missing variable in a template : define it, or use default , and fail early with an assert task that explains what is missing. Slow run : use the profile_tasks callback to list the slowest tasks, then reduce fact gathering or raise forks. - name: Stop early if required data is missing ansible.builtin.assert: that: - db_host is defined - app_port | int > 1024 fail_msg: \"db_host must be set and app_port must be above 1024\" Do not \"fix\" a failure by adding ignore_errors . That moves the problem somewhere harder to see. Predict first. A play fails with \"UNREACHABLE! ... Permission denied (publickey)\" for one host only. What is the fastest check? Your guess Answer: Run ssh -vvv with the same user and key to that host. Compare ansible_user, the key path and the authorised key on that server with the working hosts. Ansible uses ordinary SSH, so a failure there is an SSH failure first. Narrowing down a failing Ansible run Flow in order: 1, Read the error to Connection and privilege; 2, Read the error to Variables and templates; 3, Connection and privilege to Module on the target; 4, Variables and templates to Module on the target; 5, Module on the target to Fix and re-run. Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Read the error Step What it does: Note the host, task name and message type. If it fails or is weak: Skipping to retries wastes time and may repeat damage. Connection and privilege Step What it does: ssh -vvv, become settings, sudoers. If it fails or is weak: Wrong user or key looks like a random failure. Vari"}});